#### 🔍 Algorithm 1: BFS — Breadth-First Search

**File**: `backend/search/bfs.py`

**What it is**: BFS is an *uninformed* (blind) search algorithm. It uses a **queue** (First-In, First-Out) to explore all neighbors of a node at the current depth level before moving deeper. It spreads outward like ripples in water.

**How it works in this project**:
The `bfs()` function starts from the ambulance's grid position and expands outward one step at a time, adding unvisited neighbors to the queue, until it finds the hospital's location.

**Why it is used**:
BFS is used in the **Search Lab** module to act as a *baseline comparison*. It is the most straightforward algorithm and helps students visually see the difference when they compare it to smarter algorithms like A*.

**Limitation**: BFS treats every road as having equal cost and ignores road conditions (traffic, blocked roads). In an emergency city grid where roads have varying traffic costs, BFS is inefficient — it expands too many unnecessary nodes and is slow.

| Property | Value |
|---|---|
| Optimal? | ✅ Yes (for unweighted graphs) |
| Complete? | ✅ Yes |
| Space & Time | O(b^d) — can be exponential |
| Used for | Search Lab demonstration |

---

#### 🔍 Algorithm 2: DFS — Depth-First Search

**File**: `backend/search/dfs.py`

**What it is**: DFS is an *uninformed* search algorithm. It uses a **stack** (Last-In, First-Out) to explore one path as deep as possible before backtracking and trying the next path. It has a `depth_limit=50` to avoid infinite loops.

**How it works in this project**:
The `dfs()` function dives deep down one corridor of city roads until it either reaches the hospital or hits the depth limit, then backtracks and tries another direction.

**Why it is used**:
DFS is included in the Search Lab purely as an **educational demonstration** of what *not* to use for emergency routing. It proves to the professor that I have tested multiple approaches and made an informed choice.

**Limitation**: DFS does **not guarantee the shortest path**. In an emergency, sending an ambulance on a very long, winding route when a short direct route exists could cost a patient their life. This makes it completely unsuitable for the core simulation.

| Property | Value |
|---|---|
| Optimal? | ❌ No |
| Complete? | ⚠️ Only with depth limit |
| Space | O(bm) — linear |
| Used for | Search Lab demonstration (comparison) |

---

#### 🔍 Algorithm 3: UCS — Uniform Cost Search

**File**: `backend/search/ucs.py`

**What it is**: UCS is an *informed* search algorithm. It uses a **priority queue (min-heap)** ordered by the *cumulative path cost `g(n)`*. It always expands the cheapest node found so far. UCS is essentially BFS but for weighted graphs.

**How it works in this project**:
The `ucs()` function considers the actual cost of each road segment (e.g., a congested road has a higher cost than a clear road). It expands roads in order of total travel cost, guaranteeing the cheapest route is found.

**Why it is used**:
UCS is used in the Search Lab to demonstrate *cost-aware pathfinding*. Since our city grid has roads with different traffic conditions (NORMAL, CONGESTED, BLOCKED), UCS can correctly navigate around expensive congested roads that BFS would ignore.

**Limitation**: UCS explores in all directions equally (like BFS), just weighted by cost. It has no sense of *direction* toward the goal, so it still expands many unnecessary nodes in wrong directions.

| Property | Value |
|---|---|
| Optimal? | ✅ Yes (for weighted graphs) |
| Complete? | ✅ Yes |
| Space & Time | O(b^(C*/ε)) |
| Used for | Search Lab, cost-aware comparison |

---

#### 🔍 Algorithm 4: Greedy Best-First Search

**File**: `backend/search/greedy.py`

**What it is**: Greedy is an *informed* search algorithm. It uses a **priority queue** ordered by a *heuristic function `h(n)`* — the estimated distance from a node to the goal (Manhattan distance in this project). It always expands whichever node *looks closest* to the goal.

**How it works in this project**:
The `greedy()` function calculates the Manhattan distance `|row₁ - row₂| + |col₁ - col₂|` for every neighboring road intersection and always jumps toward the intersection that is physically closest to the hospital, regardless of road costs.

**Why it is used**:
Greedy is demonstrated in the Search Lab to show the *speed vs. optimality trade-off*. It finds a path very quickly because it has a strong directional pull toward the goal.

**Limitation**: Greedy can get trapped in dead-ends and does **not guarantee the shortest path**. Because it ignores actual road costs `g(n)`, it may find a fast-looking path that is actually much longer in real cost. Unacceptable for mission-critical ambulance routing.

| Property | Value |
|---|---|
| Optimal? | ❌ No |
| Complete? | ❌ No (can get stuck in loops) |
| Space & Time | O(b^m) worst case |
| Used for | Search Lab, speed demonstration |

